-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1f9e88f3a18b53230bea3a8cdb2a8c47e5e625fa33005f7644bcca265072969b" "rationale" "For x>-2 and yz>0, the partial derivatives of sqrt(x+2)+ln(sqrt(yz)) are 1/(2sqrt(x+2)), 1/(2y), and 1/(2z). This establishes original option a, matching the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading reports Correct."} {"question" "q-36144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044185/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1f9e88f3a18b53230bea3a8cdb2a8c47e5e625fa33005f7644bcca265072969b" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The product is gh=6x^4y^2-8x^2y^3. Differentiating gives the x component 24x^3y^2-16xy^3 and y component 12x^4y-24x^2y^2. Thus original option d is correct, agreeing with the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading rejected the submitted option b; this review is a derived judgment rather than a successful grade for option d." "bindings" {"problem" "7859606a992409eeb1b576374dd5ac02f94c61eb23aab912c60d49d69363760d" "worked_solution" "48fbfad2593e96bed89526d72f94bb21758f99f37e6ec461de9d6b37954453e2" "original_field" "f1a3d3549dd0111cb0c6fe3e15bb4ba4fdb36423fb5b687ac6ec743fd5182bb9" "reviewed_field" "0d4b1eff28a8cf0c57d26dbe8b6b269027655fcd64b4654e3741629faf90a9f7" "raw_widget" "73651083578a9f1c3d9046e707da16b8d155e256b745b484d64ff2b9e6a3824e"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-36144-before.png" "sha256" "9434e2d5fb1a5490d6dcd9eda94d11e6c4f807f8783a43d0622c8aa868efdd5a"} {"path" "history-q-36144.png" "sha256" "280f1338ff224139d3baa000fdc8ae580287e53911329b513bf2bd2ce600ae17"} {"path" "q-36144-after.json" "sha256" "aee8a6acbf83ad2c3cc4257ed8e064f466250d29d2b2d210a83e8b0fe64e856d"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "ac9a36a80ef0a44836f5a6868fc596ae01630b5ce4c692f2d428c887796e3e74"}]} {"question" "q-36144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }j"] ["math" "(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j"] ["math" "(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+({x}^{4}y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j"] ["math" "-16x{y}^{3}\\text{ }i+12{x}^{4}y\\text{ }j"] ["math" "24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3}+12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044185/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1f9e88f3a18b53230bea3a8cdb2a8c47e5e625fa33005f7644bcca265072969b" "rationale" "The product is gh=6x^4y^2-8x^2y^3. Differentiating gives the x component 24x^3y^2-16xy^3 and y component 12x^4y-24x^2y^2. Thus original option d is correct, agreeing with the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading rejected the submitted option b; this review is a derived judgment rather than a successful grade for option d."}] "usage" {"q-36968" [] "e-3131" [] "e-3150" [] "q-36970" [] "e-3132" [] "q-36144" [] "e-3133" [] "q-35995" [] "q-36918" []} "basis" 1641 "retractions" [[17592186124936 :question/problem "Given $f(x, y, z) = \\sqrt{xyz}$, find $\\nabla f(x, y, z)$."] [17592186124936 :question/worked-solution "Where $xyz>0$, the chain rule gives $$f_x=\\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x},\\quad f_y=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y},\\quad f_z=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}.$$ Assemble these partial derivatives: $$\\nabla f=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}2\\left(\\frac1x\\mathbf i+\\frac1y\\mathbf j+\\frac1z\\mathbf k\\right).$$"] [17592186124936 :question/answer-fields 17592186122638] [17592186124938 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla g(x, y, z)$ given that $g(x, y, z) = \\sqrt{x + 2} + \\ln (\\sqrt{yz})$."] [17592186124938 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\ln\\sqrt{yz}=\\tfrac12\\ln(yz)$ on its domain $yz>0$. For $x>-2$, $$g_x=\\frac1{2\\sqrt{x+2}},\\qquad g_y=\\frac12\\frac{z}{yz}=\\frac1{2y},\\qquad g_z=\\frac12\\frac{y}{yz}=\\frac1{2z}.$$ Hence $\\nabla g=\\frac1{2\\sqrt{x+2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac1{2y}\\mathbf j+\\frac1{2z}\\mathbf k$."] [17592186124938 :question/answer-fields 17592186122650] [17592186124860 :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y) = 3x^{2} + y^{2} - x$, find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) = \\mathbf{0} .$."] [17592186124860 :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=\\langle6x-1,2y\\rangle$. A vector is zero exactly when both components are zero, so $6x-1=0$ and $2y=0$. Thus the only point is $(\\tfrac16,0)$."] [17592186124860 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186124939 :question/problem "Given $h(x, y, z) = xy + xy^{2} + z^{3}$. Find the points $(x, y, z)$ where $\\mathrm{\\nabla } h ( x , y , z ) = \\mathbf{0} .$."] [17592186124939 :question/worked-solution "Set all three partial derivatives to zero: $$h_x=y+y^2=y(y+1)=0,\\quad h_y=x+2xy=x(1+2y)=0,\\quad h_z=3z^2=0.$$ The first equation gives $y=0$ or $y=-1$; either choice forces $x=0$ in the second equation. The third gives $z=0$. Therefore the points are $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$."] [17592186124939 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186124939 :question/answer-fields 17592186122656] [17592186124870 :question/problem "Find the gradient of $g(x,y)h(x,y)$, given $$\\begin{array}{rlrlrl}g ( x , y ) & = 3 {x}^{2} - 4 y , \\, &  & \\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( x , y ) = 6 x \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - 4 \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} , &  &  \\\\ h ( x , y ) & = 2 {x}^{2} {y}^{2} , \\, &  & \\mathrm{\\nabla } h ( x , y ) = 4 x {y}^{2} \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + 4 {x}^{2} y \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} . &  & \\end{array}$$"] [17592186124870 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\nabla(gh)=h\\nabla g+g\\nabla h$. The first component is $2x^2y^2(6x)+(3x^2-4y)(4xy^2)=24x^3y^2-16xy^3$. The second is $2x^2y^2(-4)+(3x^2-4y)(4x^2y)=12x^4y-24x^2y^2$. Thus $$\\nabla(gh)=(24x^3y^2-16xy^3)\\mathbf i+(12x^4y-24x^2y^2)\\mathbf j.$$"] [17592186124870 :question/answer-fields 17592186122242] [17592186124162 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y$."] [17592186124162 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}((x - y)\\cos y) \\\\\n&= \\cos y \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}((x - y)\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-\\cos y - (x - y)\\sin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\cos yi - (\\cos y + (x - y)\\sin y)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute\n\n$$\n(x,y)= (1,\\pi)\n$$\n\ninto the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1, \\pi ) &= f_{x}(1, \\pi )i + f_{y}(1, \\pi )j \\\\\n&= \\cos \\pi i - (\\cos \\pi  + (1 - \\pi )\\sin \\pi )j \\\\\n&=-i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124163 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$ at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz$."] [17592186124163 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{x}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 2x + yz \\\\\ng_{y}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 0 + xz \\\\\n&= xz \\\\\ng_{z}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 0 + xy \\\\\n&= xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x, y, z) &= g_{x}(x, y, z)i + g_{y}(x, y, z)j + g_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= (2x + yz)i + (xz)j + (xy)k \\\\\n\\nabla g(2,-1, 3) &= (2(2) + (-1)(3))i + 2(3)j + 2(-1)k \\\\\n&= 1i + 6j - 2k \\\\\n&= i + 6j - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124164 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2)$, find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0$."] [17592186124164 :question/worked-solution "The domain is $x>2$. The gradient is $$\\nabla f=\\left\\langle\\frac{y-4}{x-2},\\ln(x-2)\\right\\rangle.$$ Set both components equal to zero. Since $x-2\\ne0$, the first gives $y=4$. The second gives $x-2=e^0=1$, so $x=3$. Therefore the only point is $(3,4)$."] [17592186124166 :question/problem "Find the gradient of $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given $$\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=xy,&\\nabla f(x,y)&=y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j,\\\\g(x,y)&=\\sqrt{xy},&\\nabla g(x,y)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186124166 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f + f \\, \\nabla\\! g\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla [f(x, y)g(x, y)] &= g(x, y)\\nabla f(x, y) + f(x, y)\\nabla g(x, y) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{xy}(yi + xj) + xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}j) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{xy}(yi + xj) + \\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(yi + xj) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(yi + xj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122183 17592186122184 17592186122185 17592186122186 17592186122187 17592186122243 17592186122244 17592186122245 17592186122246 17592186122247 17592186122639 17592186122640 17592186122641 17592186122642 17592186122643 17592186122651 17592186122652 17592186122653 17592186122654 17592186122655 17592186122657 17592186122658 17592186122659 17592186122660 17592186122661] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122182 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122182 :answer-field/id] [17592186122182 :answer-field/key] [17592186122182 :answer-field/type] [17592186122242 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122242 :answer-field/id] [17592186122242 :answer-field/key] [17592186122242 :answer-field/type] [17592186122638 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122638 :answer-field/id] [17592186122638 :answer-field/key] [17592186122638 :answer-field/type] [17592186122650 :answer-field/c